WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.543
Diffusion runs a large network at every
step, so generation is slow.

2
00:00:06.343 --> 00:00:09.623
Neighboring steps repeat almost the same
computation.

3
00:00:09.873 --> 00:00:14.010
Caching keeps those internal features and
skips recomputing them.

4
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.540
The question is how to skip safely.

5
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.683
The obvious trick is to reuse the last
feature, or extrapolate it.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:30.454
Both assume the feature changes smoothly,
so a fitted curve should keep going.

7
00:00:30.704 --> 00:00:36.247
But early on it moves a lot, and the
forecast drifts away.

8
00:00:37.047 --> 00:00:42.041
A fixed rule has to guess a shape that
keeps changing.

9
00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:46.350
Watch the same feature over the whole
process.

10
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.714
Early on, noise is high and change is fast
and uneven.

11
00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:55.793
In the middle, change becomes small and
smooth.

12
00:00:56.043 --> 00:01:00.889
Near the end it barely moves: only details
are refined.

13
00:01:01.139 --> 00:01:03.242
One trajectory, three behaviours.

14
00:01:03.242 --> 00:01:04.819
Call them stages.

15
00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:08.619
Why should one predictor handle all three?

16
00:01:09.919 --> 00:01:16.050
A single predictor must compromise: a rule
for the noisy start fails later.

17
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.129
If stages differ, the predictor should
differ too.

18
00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:25.569
The method, LESA (learnable stage-aware
predictors), gives each stage its own

19
00:01:25.569 --> 00:01:26.041
expert.

20
00:01:26.841 --> 00:01:31.407
Each expert uses a history window that
fits its stage.

21
00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.651
Zoom into one expert.

22
00:01:34.451 --> 00:01:38.869
It receives cached features from recent
steps: the history.

23
00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:44.233
A learned linear projection mixes that
history into a vector, z.

24
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:49.569
A small network called a KAN
(Kolmogorov-Arnold network) turns the

25
00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:52.290
timestep offset into one number, alpha.

26
00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:58.633
The prediction adds a correction to the
current feature, scaled by alpha.

27
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:01.571
Training has two steps.

28
00:02:01.571 --> 00:02:04.479
First it learns from accurate features.

29
00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:09.536
At inference, its inputs are partly its
own predictions.

30
00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:14.900
If it never sees its own mistakes, small
errors build up.

31
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.386
So training feeds it its own predictions,
as at inference.

32
00:02:20.986 --> 00:02:22.980
Put it back together.

33
00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.059
One trajectory, divided into stages by
noise level.

34
00:02:27.309 --> 00:02:31.994
Each stage has its own expert, predicting
the skipped features.

35
00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:37.330
Stages, experts, predicted features: that
is how LESA accelerates diffusion.
